00:00:43.745 --> 00:00:46.245
(dance music)

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.320
- Hey guys, thank you guys so much

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.230
for caring about what's
going on right now.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.590
I just wanted to give you a small update.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:58.440
Yesterday, I did have to get surgery done.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.540
I have what's called a small
bowel obstruction, or I had.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.880
It was formed by a scar tissue

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.800
from my previous surgeries that I had

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.270
before I started YouTube.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:13.220
- You wanna talk about
this Nasogastric tube, pSN?

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.053
- Yeah.

00:01:14.053 --> 00:01:16.380
As you can imagine, if there's
a blockage in the bowel,

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.530
there's no place for food
or liquids to pass through.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:23.240
And even if somebody isn't
eating anything by mouth,

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:24.290
the stomach for instance

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:27.370
is naturally screening a lot of fluid.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:28.577
And even if you think about our mouth

00:01:28.577 --> 00:01:29.930
and the saliva that we make

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:32.940
that we're constantly swallowing,
that has no place to go.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.910
This tube serves to
suction out that liquid

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:38.980
so that it actually has a place to go.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.248
And it doesn't just build up.

00:01:40.248 --> 00:01:41.590
- It sounded like he was saying

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:43.740
was that he was having the
small bowel obstruction

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.920
to translate into common English terms

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:46.953
is his small intestine

00:01:46.953 --> 00:01:50.190
which comes right off of the
stomach, had an obstruction.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.610
He explained that it was
from his prior surgery.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.440
There was probably some
scar tissue that essentially

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.870
closed off the intestine, preventing food

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.110
and the liquids from traveling down.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:02.980
And as a result, you
start vomiting profusely.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:04.480
You feel awful.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.100
Your stomach starts expanding.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:06.933
You're bloating.

00:02:06.933 --> 00:02:07.766
You're uncomfortable.

00:02:07.766 --> 00:02:09.625
No matter what medicine
you give to a patient,

00:02:09.625 --> 00:02:12.650
unless you either open up the blockage

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:14.910
or take all the contents

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.690
of the stomach and the GI
system out of the body,

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.430
you're not gonna stop vomiting.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.440
So putting in these NG tubes in patients

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.690
like this was the number one
way to relieve the symptoms

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.227
and to buy time in order to
get them to the procedure.

00:02:28.227 --> 00:02:31.713
- Hey guys, I am at
the hospital right now.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.360
I'm fine.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.000
I've got an NG tube in my nose right now

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.640
which drains stuff out of my stomach

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.950
because I have a small intestine blockage.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:45.300
Man, this is unexpected.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.430
Came out of nowhere, but I'm fine.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:48.963
It's nothing life threatening.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.700
I don't even think I'm gonna
need surgery this time.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.220
They're just going to suck it out of me.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:55.920
And hopefully that fixes the problem.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.130
- In five years, two
small bowel obstructions.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.120
Is that common?

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.860
I mean, I know there are recurrences

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:06.620
especially once you've had an
intestinal obstruction before

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.030
and surgery and adhesions,
but in his age, is that,

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.380
have you seen patients like this?

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:12.213
Is it often?

00:03:12.213 --> 00:03:13.490
- You know, I've seen patients like this,

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:15.943
but there's no predicting
when about obstruction

00:03:15.943 --> 00:03:18.200
can happen again after it's happened once.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.270
If it's due to the scar tissue
that we were talking about.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:24.410
Sounds like he might not need
surgery this time around.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:27.530
So I don't exactly know
if it's the same cause

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:28.790
of obstruction again.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:30.270
I wonder if there's
something else going on.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.690
Like maybe something we call an ileus

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:34.770
where the intestine is just slow to move.

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.289
And in that case, it just needs time

00:03:36.289 --> 00:03:37.690
to kind of get going again.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:42.040
And we don't wanna add
more food or liquid to it.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.050
And it will sort of, we have to figure out

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:45.350
why that ileus is happening

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.140
but you know, a little
bowel rest might help.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.457
- This is cool for the audience to know.

00:03:49.457 --> 00:03:52.780
They're probably wondering if
he can't eat, he can't drink,

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.610
how is he supposed to stay hydrated?

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:56.460
How is he supposed to get calories?

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.777
You wanna take us through
the ways that we can do that?

00:03:58.777 --> 00:04:00.840
- That's the main way we do that really

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.540
is through the IV, through a vein.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:05.750
We're able to provide fluids that way.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.800
We're able to sometimes
even provide nutrition.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.070
Some people have issues that prevent them

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.610
from being able to eat for months on end.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:16.400
And sometimes people have
to be on IV nutrition

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:17.410
for a long time.

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:18.730
- I've got good news.

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:20.770
The NG tube has been taken out

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:23.640
of my nose and my gut
because that's what it does.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.540
We did a bunch of tests this morning.

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:27.385
A lot of x-rays and a lot of scans

00:04:27.385 --> 00:04:30.830
and some contrast to see if
things were moving through me.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.850
And it seemed like things
were moving through okay.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:34.760
Which is good.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:36.830
So the doc said we're gonna take it out

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:39.270
and observe a little longer.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:40.880
I'm still not allowed to eat anything.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:44.440
But the doc said that if I
can have a bowel movement

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:45.950
then everything is on the up and up.

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.330
- We're waiting for the bowel movement.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:48.980
We're waiting for the flatulence.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:50.320
It's funny because literally

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.500
as a med student and
as a resident early on,

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.710
you're documenting like
no flatulence as of yet.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.530
Like you're waiting for that moment.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:58.450
What's interesting to me

00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:00.580
is he mentioned the
contrast that they're doing

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:02.240
I guess, like swallow studies

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.760
or is that something you
usually do with SBOs?

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:06.593
- Yeah.

00:05:06.593 --> 00:05:07.780
I think that if we're suspecting

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.280
that it's gonna clear up on its own,

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.810
then we can get an x-ray and
have them drink contrast.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.560
And that contrast will
basically be traced through

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:17.218
the intestine and make
sure it's getting past

00:05:17.218 --> 00:05:19.223
the point of blockage.

00:05:19.223 --> 00:05:21.540
- [Man] This is a tweet that
came out the following day.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:22.930
- All right, I'm ready for the tweet.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:23.763
Let's open it.

00:05:23.763 --> 00:05:24.783
Oh, he pooped.

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:29.720
Actually I think I saw this trending.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.067
Didn't this trend on Twitter?

00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:32.457
- [Man] #hepooped.

00:05:32.457 --> 00:05:34.290
- #hepooped.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:36.410
Isn't it funny to
celebrate a bowel movement?

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:37.930
Like people don't think that that happens

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:39.820
but doctors celebrate poops.

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:40.776
What do we got?

00:05:40.776 --> 00:05:42.900
- [Man] The tweets inspired some memes.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.290
- Okay, we got some memes.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:45.390
Congratulations.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:49.090
This certificate is to
commend Mark Edward Fishbach

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:51.950
for passing a bowel movement award

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:53.383
